var definitions = ['<h2>Un homme Important</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the masculine singular indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a/an" in English. It is used here to introduce a noun.</div><div><strong>homme</strong> This is the French word for "man". It is a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>Important</strong> This is an adjective in French that means "important". It is capitalized here, likely because it is part of a title or heading. When used as an adjective in a sentence, it must agree in gender and number with the noun it modifies, but in this context, it seems to be a part of a proper name or a title.</div>','<h2>Un enfant</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English, indicating a singular, non-specific item.</div><div><strong>enfant</strong> This is a noun in French that means "child". It is masculine and singular, which is why it is preceded by the masculine singular indefinite article "un".</div>','<h2>Un avion</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It indicates that the following noun is indefinite.</div><div><strong>avion</strong> This is the French word for "airplane" or "plane". It is a masculine singular noun referring to an aircraft.</div>','<h2>Il y a un garçon dans la piscine.</h2><div><strong>Il y a</strong> This phrase translates to "there is" or "there are" in English and is used to indicate the existence or presence of someone or something.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>garçon</strong> This is a French noun meaning "boy". It is in the singular form and follows the masculine article "un".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition that translates to "in" in English, indicating the location of someone or something.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, translated as "the" in English. "Piscine" is feminine, so it uses "la" rather than "le".</div><div><strong>piscine</strong> This is the French word for "swimming pool". It is a feminine singular noun.</div>','<h2>Une femme</h2><div><strong>Une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine nouns in the singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>femme</strong> This is a French noun meaning "woman". It is feminine and singular to match the article "une".</div>','<h2>Une orange</h2><div><strong>Une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It’s used here to introduce a singular noun that is feminine.</div><div><strong>orange</strong> This is a French feminine noun meaning "orange". It refers to both the color and the fruit in French, just as in English.</div>','<h2>Une chaise</h2><div><strong>Une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It is used here to introduce a singular, non-specific item.</div><div><strong>chaise</strong> This is the French word for "chair". It is a feminine noun, which is why it is preceded by the feminine indefinite article "une".</div>','<h2>Il y a une personne dans la salle.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, usually translating to "he" in English. In this context, "Il" doesn’t refer to a person but is a part of the impersonal expression "Il y a", which means "there is" or "there are".</div><div><strong>y</strong> "Y" is a French adverb of place that can mean "there" in English. In the expression "Il y a", it is always used with the verb "avoir" to indicate existence or presence.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have" in English. When used in the expression "Il y a", it forms a construction that translates to "there is" or "there are".</div><div><strong>une personne</strong> This phrase means "one person" or "a person" in English. "Une" is a feminine singular indefinite article equivalent to "a" or "an", and "personne" is a feminine singular noun that means "person".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition that means "in" in English. It is used to indicate location or position.</div><div><strong>la salle</strong> This phrase means "the room" in English. "La" is the feminine singular definite article meaning "the", and "salle" is a feminine singular noun that means "room" or "hall".</div>','<h2>Des filles</h2><div><strong>Des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, equivalent to "some" or "any" in English. It is used before plural nouns when the exact number is not specified.</div><div><strong>filles</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "girls". It is the plural form of "fille", which means "girl" in English. The word "filles" is feminine and plural to match the gender and number of the noun it describes.</div>','<h2>Des garçons</h2><div><strong>Des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, which is used before a plural noun. It is the combination of "de" (of/from) and "les" (the). In this context, it translates to "some" in English, indicating a non-specific quantity.</div><div><strong>garçons</strong> This is a French noun meaning "boys". It’s in the plural form to correspond with the plural article "des".</div>','<h2>Des fleurs</h2><div><strong>Des</strong> This is a partitive article in French used to express an unspecified quantity, it can be translated to "some" in English. It is often used with plural nouns when the exact number is not stated or important.</div><div><strong>fleurs</strong> This is the plural form of the French word "fleur", which means "flower". It indicates more than one flower.</div>','<h2>Des billes</h2><div><strong>Des</strong> This is the plural form of the indefinite article in French, which is used before plural nouns when the exact number is not specified. It is equivalent to "some" in English.</div><div><strong>billets</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "bille", which means "marble" in English. It refers to the small, round glass or clay balls used in various children’s games.</div>','<h2>Je ne veux pas de gâteau.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is the negation particle used in French to form a negation. It is part of the two-part negation system, typically used before the verb.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates the subject’s desire or lack thereof.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the two-part negation in French. Together with "ne", it negates the action of the verb. In this case, it follows the verb to complete the negation.</div><div><strong>de</strong> In this context, "de" is a part of the negation construction used before nouns. It can be thought of as "any" or "not any". After negation, "des" (some) turns into "de" or "d’ ".</div><div><strong>gâteau</strong> This is a French noun meaning "cake". In this sentence, it is the object of the sentence—the thing that the subject does not desire.</div>','<h2>Il n’a pas d’animaux sur sa ferme.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual or a gender-neutral subject.</div><div><strong>n’a pas</strong> This is a negation phrase in French, made up of "ne" and "pas" which wrap around the verb "a" (has), which is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir" (to have). Together, they express the negative "does not have". Note that in spoken or informal written French, the "ne" is often omitted, thus "n’a pas" is frequently seen as "a pas".</div><div><strong>d’animaux</strong> This is the plural form of "d’animal", which means "of/any animals". "D’" is used before a vowel sound and is a contraction of the preposition "de" (of/from) with the plural article "des" (some), resulting in the meaning "any animals" or "some animals".</div><div><strong>sur sa</strong> This phrase combines the preposition "sur" (on) with the possessive adjective "sa" (his/her/its). "Sa" agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which in this case is the feminine noun "ferme". It denotes that the ownership or possession of something, in this case, the farm belongs to the subject "he".</div><div><strong>ferme</strong> This is a French noun meaning "farm". It is feminine and singular, which is why the possessive "sa" is used before it.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Il est un petit garçon.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It indicates the state of being of the subject.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite masculine singular article, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>petit</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "small" or "little". Here, it’s used before a masculine singular noun to describe size.</div><div><strong>garçon</strong> This is a French noun meaning "boy". It is masculine and in the singular form to refer to one boy.</div>','<h2>Y a-t-il un problème?</h2><div><strong>Y</strong> This is a pronoun that generally stands for "there" in English and is used to indicate the existence or presence of something.</div><div><strong>a-t-il</strong> This is a phrase composed of three parts: "a" is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have", used here as an auxiliary to form a question. "t" is a euphonic letter inserted to make the pronunciation easier when the verb ends with a vowel and is followed by a pronoun beginning with a vowel, thus avoiding a clash of vowels. "il" is the third-person singular subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" or "it" in English.</div><div><strong>un problème</strong> This phrase translates to "a problem". "Un" is the masculine singular form of the indefinite article, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English, and "problème" is a masculine noun meaning "problem".</div>','<h2>Oui. Je t’enverrai un texto.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It indicates agreement or affirmation.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>t’</strong> This is a contraction of "te" before a vowel or silent "h", which is the direct or indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in the informal singular form. In this context, it is part of the pronominal construction "t’enverrai" which means "I will send to you".</div><div><strong>enverrai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense of the verb "envoyer", which means "to send". Here it is combined with the pronoun "te" to form "t’enverrai", meaning "I will send to you".</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>texto</strong> This is a informal term for a text message in French. It is a borrowed word from English "text" with "-o" added, following a pattern in some French slang. It is masculine and singular to agree with the indefinite article "un".</div>','<h2>Est-ce un gros problème?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce</strong> This is a contraction of "Est-ce que", which is a common French phrase used to introduce a question. It can be translated as "Is it" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" in English.</div><div><strong>gros</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "big" or "large". In this sentence, it modifies the noun "problème", agreeing with it in the masculine singular form.</div><div><strong>problème?</strong> This is a French noun meaning "problem". It is masculine and singular to match the indefinite article "un" and the adjective "gros".</div>','<h2>Tu devrais te reposer un peu.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>devrais</strong> This word is the second-person singular conditional tense of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". Here, it suggests a recommendation or advice, meaning "should".</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun in French. It corresponds to "yourself" in the reflexive construction "te reposer", which means "to rest". It reflects the action of the verb back onto the subject.</div><div><strong>reposer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb which means "to rest". In the construction "te reposer", it’s used reflexively, meaning "to rest yourself".</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> These words translate to "a little" or "a bit" in English. They indicate the extent or duration of the suggested rest.</div>','<h2>Non, je voudrais un aller-retour.</h2><div><strong>Non</strong> This is the French word for "no". It negates a statement or responds negatively to a question.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a polite desire or request, similar to "would like" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French masculine indefinite article, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes a masculine noun.</div><div><strong>aller-retour</strong> This is a French compound noun, meaning "round trip" in English. It’s used when referring to a ticket that allows someone to go to a destination and return from it.</div>','<h2>Il y a un chien dans la maison.</h2><div><strong>Il y a</strong> This is a French phrase used to express the existence of something, equivalent to "there is" or "there are" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>chien</strong> This is a French noun meaning "dog". It is masculine and in its singular form here.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in" or "inside".</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used before the word "maison" which is feminine.</div><div><strong>maison</strong> This is a French noun meaning "house". It is feminine and in its singular form here.</div>','<h2>Ce doit être un travail très prenant alors.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, which can be translated to "it" or "this" in English. It typically refers to a previously mentioned or understood subject or object.</div><div><strong>doit</strong> This is the third-person singular present form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "to have to" in English. It expresses an obligation or a strong likelihood.</div><div><strong>être</strong> This is an infinitive form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. Here, it is used after "doit" to form the phrase "must be".</div><div><strong>un travail</strong> This phrase means "a job" or "work" in English. "Un" is the indefinite article "a" for masculine nouns, and "travail" is the noun for job or work.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It is used to intensify the adjective that comes after it.</div><div><strong>prenant</strong> This is a present participle of the verb "prendre", which means "to take", but in this context, "prenant" is used as an adjective, meaning "engrossing" or "time-consuming" in English.</div><div><strong>alors</strong> This word can have several meanings depending on the context, including "so", "then", or "therefore". In this sentence, it likely means "then" or "so", indicating a conclusion or inference based on what has been said beforehand.</div>','<h2>Il y aura un édifice là-bas.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the French pronoun for "he" or "it". In this context, it serves as an impersonal pronoun, similar to the English "it" when talking about the weather or time.</div><div><strong>y</strong> This is a French adverbial pronoun that translates to "there" in English. It often replaces a noun that has been introduced by the preposition "à" or "en", and in this sentence, it is used to indicate location.</div><div><strong>aura</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used to indicate that something "will have" or "will happen" in the future, functioning similarly to "there will be" in English.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the masculine singular indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>édifice</strong> This is a French noun that translates to "building" or "edifice" in English. It is used to refer to a structure with a roof and walls.</div><div><strong>là-bas</strong> This is a French adverb phrase, meaning "over there". It indicates a more specific location, typically at some distance from the speaker.</div>','<h2>Nous envoyions un cadeau à nos amis.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, meaning "we". It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>envoyions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "envoyer", which means "to send". It indicates a continuous or repeated action in the past.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French masculine singular indefinite article, meaning "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>cadeau</strong> This is a French noun meaning "gift" or "present". It is masculine and singular to match the article "un".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "to" in English. It is used here to indicate the recipient of the gift.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the French possessive adjective meaning "our". It is plural to agree with "amis", indicating possession by the subject "nous".</div><div><strong>amis</strong> This is the French word for "friends". It is masculine plural to agree with "nos" and is the object of the preposition "à", representing the recipients of the gift.</div>','<h2>Oui, pour un coût additionnel.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition that means "for" in English. It is often used to indicate purpose or to specify the use of something.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French masculine singular indefinite article, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It introduces a non-specific noun.</div><div><strong>coût</strong> This is the French word for "cost" or "price". It refers to the expense associated with purchasing goods or services.</div><div><strong>additionnel</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "additional". It describes something that is added or extra. In the sentence, it agrees in gender and number with "coût" which is masculine singular.</div>','<h2>Oui, nous avons un stationnement souterrain.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is used to affirmatively respond to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it indicates possession.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in French, used here in the masculine singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>stationnement</strong> This is a French noun meaning "parking". In this context, it refers to a parking facility.</div><div><strong>souterrain</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "underground". Here, it’s used after the noun to describe the type of parking facility being referred to.</div>','<h2>Oui, justement, j’ai un examen ce matin!</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s used to affirm a statement or respond affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>justement</strong> This word can mean "exactly" or "just". In the context of this sentence, it emphasizes the timing or relevance of the statement that follows.</div><div><strong>j’</strong> This is the contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, when followed by a vowel sound. It’s equivalent to "I" in English and serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it’s used to form the present tense.</div><div><strong>un examen</strong> This phrase means "an exam". "Un" is the indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, and "examen" is a masculine singular noun meaning "exam" or "test".</div><div><strong>ce matin</strong> This phrase translates to "this morning". "Ce" is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this", and "matin" is a masculine singular noun meaning "morning".</div>','<h2>Je me reposais sur un matelas gonflable.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This word is a reflexive pronoun, which relates back to the subject and is part of a reflexive verb construction in French. Here, it is used with "reposais" to indicate that the subject is performing the action on themselves.</div><div><strong>reposais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the reflexive verb "se reposer", which means "to rest". The imperfect tense is used to describe past actions that were ongoing or habitual.</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "on". It is used here to indicate the location relative to the matelas gonflable.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This word is the masculine singular form of the indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>matelas</strong> This is a French noun meaning "mattress". In this sentence, it is the direct object receiving the action of the verb "reposais".</div><div><strong>gonflable</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "inflatable". Here, it is used to describe the noun "matelas". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, so it is in the masculine singular form as "matelas" is masculine and singular.</div>','<h2>Ils nageront professionnellement un jour.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating a group of people.</div><div><strong>nageront</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "nager", which means "to swim". By using the future tense, it indicates that the action will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>professionnellement</strong> This is an adverb meaning "professionally". It describes the manner in which the verb action will be performed.</div><div><strong>un jour</strong> This phrase translates to "one day" in English, and is used to specify an undetermined point in the future when the action will take place.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que je peux ouvrir un des pots de bonbons?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a French phrase used to indicate that what follows is a question. It is roughly equivalent to "is it that" in English, though it isn’t typically translated word for word.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "can" or "to be able to". It indicates ability or permission.</div><div><strong>ouvrir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French meaning "to open". It’s used here directly after the conjugated verb "peux", following the structure "can (verb)".</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French masculine singular indefinite article, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a partitive article in French usually translated as "some" in English. It is used here to mean "one of the" when combined with "un".</div><div><strong>pots</strong> This is the French word for "jars" or "pots". It’s a masculine noun in the plural form.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in French that can have various meanings, including "of" or "from". Here, it’s used as part of the partitive article "des" in combination with "un" to mean "one of the".</div><div><strong>bonbons?</strong> This is the French word for "candies" or "sweets". It’s a masculine noun in the plural form and is the object of the verb "ouvrir".</div>','<h2>Un parapluie est différent des autres.</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It introduces the noun "parapluie".</div><div><strong>parapluie</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French, meaning "umbrella". It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be" in English. It links the subject "parapluie" to its descriptor.</div><div><strong>différent</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "different". In this context, it describes the umbrella, agreeing with the masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" and the definite article "les", which together translate to "from the" or "of the" in English. It introduces the plural noun that the umbrella is being compared to.</div><div><strong>autres</strong> This is the plural form of "autre", a French adjective meaning "other". Here, it’s used as a noun meaning "others", referring to other umbrellas.</div>','<h2>J’habite dans une maison.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> The contraction of "je" (I in English) before a vowel or mute h, in this case, "habite". This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>habite</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "habiter", which means "to live". It indicates where the subject resides or is settled.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that can be translated to "in" or "inside" in English. It’s used here to specify the location.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, which translates as "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>maison</strong> This is a French noun meaning "house". It’s feminine and singular to match the article "une".</div>','<h2>Une sorte de bureau de poste?</h2><div><strong>Une</strong> This is the feminine singular indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It’s used here to introduce the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>sorte</strong> This is the French word for "kind" or "type". It’s part of a commonly used expression that means "a kind of" or "a sort of" in English.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that is often translated as "of" in English. In the expression "une sorte de", it is used to link "sorte" to the noun that it describes.</div><div><strong>bureau</strong> This is the French word for "office" or "desk". Here, it refers to a place of business or service, such as a post office.</div><div><strong>de poste</strong> This phrase means "post" or "mail" in English. When combined with "bureau", it forms the term for "post office". "De" serves as a link between "bureau" and "poste", creating the compound noun "bureau de poste", which means "post office".</div><div>So, the entire phrase <strong>Une sorte de bureau de poste?</strong> translates to "A kind of post office?" in English. It’s a question someone might ask when trying to classify a type of establishment they are not familiar with.</div>','<h2>Vous écriviez une lettre</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used here as the subject of the sentence and can also denote formal address or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>écriviez</strong> This is the imperfect tense form of the verb "écrire", which means "to write". It describes an ongoing past action without a specified completion, similar to "were writing" or "used to write" in English.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>lettre</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French meaning "letter". It is the direct object of the verb in this sentence, indicating what is being written.</div>','<h2>Ce n’est pas une bonne histoire.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> is a demonstrative pronoun in French that can often be translated as "this" or "it". It’s the subject of the sentence and is used to refer to a thing or situation that’s already been mentioned or is about to be described.</div><div><strong>n’est pas</strong> is a negation formed by the contraction of "ne" and "est", which is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", meaning "to be". The "pas" completes the negation. Together, they convey "is not" and negate the statement.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article in French used for feminine singular nouns, which translates to "a" or "an" in English. It’s used here to introduce the noun "histoire".</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This word is a French adjective meaning "good". It’s in the feminine singular form to agree with "histoire". Adjectives in French must match the gender and number of the noun they modify.</div><div><strong>histoire</strong> This is a feminine singular noun that means "story" or "history" in English. In this context, it’s used to mean "story".</div>','<h2>On dirait une infection aux poumons.</h2><div><strong>On</strong> In this context, the French pronoun "on" can mean "one", "we", "you", or "they". It is often used for general statements or observations, similar to "it seems" or "it looks like" in English.</div><div><strong>dirait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense form of the verb "dire", which means "to say" or "to tell". In this sentence, it is used together with "on" to express an observation or assumption, translating to "it looks like" or "one would say".</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite feminine singular article meaning "a" or "an" in English. It is used here to introduce the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>infection</strong> A French noun meaning "infection" in English. It is feminine and is the main object of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aux</strong> This is the preposition "à" combined with the plural definite article "les". In English, this combination translates to "to the" or "at the". In the context of this sentence, "aux" is used to designate the location of the infection, implying "in the" when referring to body parts.</div><div><strong>poumons</strong> This is a French noun meaning "lungs" in English. It is in the plural form to match the plural article "les" within "aux". "Poumons" is the location of the infection mentioned in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Ils chantaient dans une chorale.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the French third-person plural pronoun, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of males or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>chantaient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "chanter", which means "to sing". This tense is used to describe an ongoing or repeated action in the past.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition that means "in" or "inside". It indicates the location or the setting where the action is taking place.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, and it translates to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>chorale</strong> This French word means "choir". It’s a feminine singular noun referring to a group of singers who perform together, typically singing harmonized arrangements.</div>','<h2>Oui. Il y a une visite tous les matins.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is an affirmative response to a question or a statement.</div><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the impersonal third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "it" in English or somewhat like "there" in expressions like "there is". Here, it’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>y a</strong> This comes from the verb "avoir", which means "to have" in English. In this construction "il y a", it translates to "there is" or "there are". It is used to indicate the existence of something.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>visite</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French, which means "visit" or "tour". Here, it refers to a guided tour or a visit.</div><div><strong>tous</strong> This word is a plural adjective in French meaning "all" or "every". It is used to refer to a recurring event or situation.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article for plural nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>matins</strong> This is the plural form of "matin", which is a masculine noun meaning "morning" in English. It indicates that the visits occur every morning.</div>','<h2>Nous habitons dans une très belle maison.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>habitons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "habiter", which means "to live" or "to reside". It indicates the action being taken by the subject.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition meaning "in" or "within" in English. It introduces the place where the action of living is occurring.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This word translates to "a" or "an" in English. It’s the feminine singular indefinite article used before a noun.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "very". It is used to modify the adjective that follows it, intensifying its meaning.</div><div><strong>belle</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "beautiful". It is in the feminine singular form to agree with the noun it modifies. If the noun were masculine, the adjective would be "beau".</div><div><strong>maison</strong> This is a French noun meaning "house". It is feminine singular here, so all its modifiers must also match this gender and number.</div>','<h2>J’aimerais louer une chambre d’hôtel.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contracted form of "je", which is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. The contraction is used before a vowel or a mute "h" for ease of pronunciation.</div><div><strong>aimerais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". In this context, it means "would like". When combined with "je", it expresses a polite desire or wish.</div><div><strong>louer</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French meaning "to rent" or "to hire". It’s used here to indicate the action the speaker would like to perform.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This word is the feminine singular form of the indefinite article in French, translating to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes a singular feminine noun.</div><div><strong>chambre</strong> This is a French noun meaning "room". In this context, it refers specifically to a "hotel room".</div><div><strong>d’hôtel</strong> This is a possessive phrase composed of "de", meaning "of" with the contraction of "de + le", and "hôtel", which means "hotel". It specifies the type of room being rented—it’s a room of a hotel. Note that "d’hôtel" is together due to the French rule of contracting "de" and "le" into "d’" before a vowel sound.</div>','<h2>Savez-vous s’il y a une correspondance?</h2><div><strong>Savez-vous</strong> This is a formal way to say "do you know" in French. It is a combination of "savez", the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "savoir" (to know), and the pronoun "vous", which means "you".</div><div><strong>s’il</strong> This is a contraction of "si" (if) and "il" (he/it), it often introduces a condition or a doubt in French.</div><div><strong>y a</strong> This comes from the verb "avoir" (to have) and it means "there is" or "there are" when used in this form. "Y" usually refers to a previously mentioned or implied place.</div><div><strong>une correspondance</strong> This means "a connection" in the context of transport, referring to a transfer from one vehicle to another in order to continue a journey.</div><div>The entire sentence translates to "Do you know if there is a connection?" which is typically asked when trying to figure out if one can transfer from one mode of transport to another to reach their destination.</div></div>','<h2>Je voudrais une chambre au rez-de-chaussée.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". In this case, it expresses a polite request, similar to "would like" in English.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article in French, used here for a feminine singular noun. It translates to "a" in English.</div><div><strong>chambre</strong> This is the French word for "room" or "bedroom". It is the direct object of the verb "voudrais" in this sentence, indicating what the speaker would like.</div><div><strong>au</strong> This is a contraction of "à le" in French. "À" means "to" or "at", and "le" is the definite article "the". Together, "au" is used before masculine singular nouns to indicate location or direction, and translates as "at the" or "to the" in English.</div><div><strong>rez-de-chaussée</strong> This is the French term for "ground floor". The expression literally translates to "level of the ground" and refers to the floor at ground level, typically the first floor in French-speaking countries.</div>','<h2>Je mange une pomme.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating who is performing the action.</div><div><strong>mange</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "manger", which means "to eat". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" in English. It’s used here to introduce a single, unspecific object.</div><div><strong>pomme</strong> This is a French noun meaning "apple". It’s feminine and singular, matching the article "une". It’s the object of the verb "mange", indicating what is being eaten.</div>','<h2>Prenez une pilule, trois fois par jour.</h2><div><strong>Prenez</strong> This is the imperative form of the verb "prendre", which means "to take" in English. It is used here to give a command or instruction, addressing the audience directly with the formal "you" (vous).</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article for feminine nouns in the singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>pilule</strong> This is a French noun meaning "pill". It is a feminine noun.</div><div><strong>trois</strong> This is the number "three" in French. It indicates the quantity or frequency.</div><div><strong>fois</strong> This is a French noun meaning "times". It is often used to describe repetition or frequency of an action.</div><div><strong>par</strong> This is a preposition that is equivalent to "by" or "per" in English. It’s used here to give a sense of distribution or frequency.</div><div><strong>jour</strong> This is a French noun meaning "day". Here it denotes the time frame for taking the pill.</div>','<h2>Nous serons en bas dans une minute.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>serons</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense of the verb "être", which means "to be". It indicates a future action or state relating to the subject.</div><div><strong>en bas</strong> This phrase means "downstairs" or "down" in English. It indicates the location where the subject will be.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This preposition translates to "in" when referring to time in English. It’s used to specify the time frame within which an action will take place.</div><div><strong>une minute</strong> This means "one minute" in English. It gives the exact duration after which the event (the subject being downstairs) is expected to occur.</div>','<h2>Il prendra des vacances dans une semaine.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>prendra</strong> is the future tense form of the verb "prendre", which means "to take". In this sentence, it is conjugated to agree with "il" as the third-person singular, meaning "he will take".</div><div><strong>des vacances</strong> means "vacations" or "a holiday" in English. It is in the plural form and it is the direct object of the verb "prendra".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> is a preposition meaning "in" or "within" in English. It is used here to indicate a period of time before an event occurs.</div><div><strong>une</strong> is an indefinite article in French, meaning "a" or "an" in English. It is in the feminine singular form to agree with "semaine".</div><div><strong>semaine</strong> means "week" in English. It is a feminine singular noun and, when combined with "dans" and "une", it forms the phrase "in a week" to specify when the vacations will start.</div>','<h2>Il nous a donné des fleurs.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "us" in English. It’s the indirect object of the sentence, meaning the action is being done to "us".</div><div><strong>a donné</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "donner", which means "to give". The "a" indicates the auxiliary verb "avoir" in the passé composé, creating the past tense. "donné" is the past participle of "donner".</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, equivalent to "some" in English. It is used here before a plural noun to which it refers.</div><div><strong>fleurs</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "fleur", which means "flower" in English. It’s the direct object of the sentence, receiving the action of the verb.</div>','<h2>Il y a des chats là-bas.</h2><div><strong>Il y a</strong> This is a common French phrase that can be translated as "there is" or "there are". It’s used to indicate the existence or presence of something.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, which is used before plural nouns when the exact number is not specified. It can be translated as "some" or "any" in English.</div><div><strong>chats</strong> This is the French word for "cats". It’s the plural form of "chat", which means "cat".</div><div><strong>là-bas</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "over there" or "that way". It’s used to indicate a location that is somewhat distant from both the speaker and the listener.</div>','<h2>Elles doivent acheter des fruits.</h2><div><strong>Elles</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English when referring to a group of females or a mixed gender group. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doivent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". It indicates an obligation or necessity for the subject to perform the action.</div><div><strong>acheter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to buy" in French. It follows the auxiliary verb "doivent" to specify what action the subject needs to carry out.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a partitive article in French, often used before plural nouns to denote an unspecified quantity, equivalent to "some" in English.</div><div><strong>fruits</strong> This is a French word for "fruits". It’s a plural noun and is the direct object of the verb "acheter", referring to what the subject needs to buy.</div>','<h2>Bonjour! Je recherche des chaussures!</h2><div><strong>Bonjour</strong> This is the French greeting for "good morning" or "hello". It’s a universal greeting used in French-speaking regions.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>recherche</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "rechercher", which means "to search for" or "to look for". It indicates the action the subject is undertaking.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, used before plural nouns when the quantity is not specified. It is equivalent to "some" in English.</div><div><strong>chaussures</strong> This is the French word for "shoes". It is a feminine, plural noun.</div>','<h2>Il y avait des canards là-bas.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the French impersonal pronoun "it". In this context, "il" does not refer to a male subject but is used in a general sense to mean "there".</div><div><strong>y avait</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the verb "avoir" in the imperfect tense, which means "there was" or "there were" in English. It is used to describe ongoing states or repeated actions in the past. In this case, it suggests that the ducks were present in the past.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a French article that is used in the plural form and is equivalent to "some" or "any" in English. It indicates that there is an undefined number of ducks.</div><div><strong>canards</strong> This is a French noun meaning "ducks". It is in the plural form to correspond with the plural article "des".</div><div><strong>là-bas</strong> This is a French adverbial phrase meaning "over there" or "there" in English. It specifies the location of the ducks mentioned.</div>','<h2>Nous nous faisons des biscuits.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and is used twice here. The first "Nous" is the actual subject pronoun, while the second "nous" is a reflexive pronoun, indicating that the action is performed by and upon the subject.</div><div><strong>nous faisons</strong> This is a conjugation of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make" in English. In this form, "faisons" is in the first-person plural present tense, indicating "we do" or "we make".</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, used here to mean "some". It’s used before plural nouns when the exact quantity is not specified.</div><div><strong>biscuits</strong> This is the French word for "biscuits" or "cookies". It is a plural noun, matching the plural article "des".</div>','<h2>Oui, je peux vous donner des antidouleurs.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is used to affirmatively respond to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the speaker’s ability to perform an action.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used here as an indirect object, indicating the person to whom the action is being done.</div><div><strong>donner</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to give" in French. In combination with "peux" (from "pouvoir"), it refers to the speaker’s ability to give something.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a plural article in French, here it is used as part of the partitive construction which means "some" or "any" in English. It indicates an unspecified quantity of the following noun.</div><div><strong>antidouleurs</strong> This is the French word for "painkillers" or "analgesics". It’s a plural noun referring to medications that are used to relieve pain.</div>','<h2>Tu achèteras des tomates pour le souper.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>achèteras</strong> This is the second-person singular future tense form of the verb "acheter", meaning "to buy". This indicates a future action that will be performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>des tomates</strong> This phrase translates to "some tomatoes" in English. "Des" is a partitive article used here to indicate an unspecified quantity of tomatoes, which is a plural noun.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It introduces the purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>le souper</strong> This phrase translates to "the supper" or "the dinner". "Le" is the masculine singular definite article "the", and "souper" is a French noun that refers to the evening meal.</div>','<h2>Tu lançais des feuilles dans les airs.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lançais</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "lancer", which means "to throw". It describes a past action that was ongoing or repeated.</div><div><strong>des feuilles</strong> This phrase means "leaves". "Des" is an indefinite article used in French for plural nouns, and "feuilles" is the plural form of "feuille", which means "leaf".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition that means "in" or "into", indicating the direction of the action within the sentence.</div><div><strong>les airs</strong> This phrase translates to "the air" in English. "Les" is the definite article for plural nouns in French, and "airs" is the plural form of "air", which means "air". In this context, it indicates that the leaves were thrown upwards into the air.</div>','<h2>Vous donniez des fleurs à votre père.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is also used as a formal second-person singular pronoun. In this sentence, it’s the subject.</div><div><strong>donniez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "donner" in the imperfect tense, which means "to give". In the imperfect, it denotes an ongoing action or a habitual action in the past; here, it suggests you were giving on a regular basis or during a moment in the past.</div><div><strong>des fleurs</strong> This translates to "some flowers" or "flowers" in English. "Des" is an indefinite article used in French for plural nouns. "Fleurs" is the plural form of "fleur", which means "flower". Together, they function as the direct object of the verb "donniez".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is the French preposition that can mean "to" or "at", among other meanings. It is used here to indicate the indirect object of the action.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective which means "your" in English. It shows ownership or relation to the subject "vous", reflecting formality or plurality.</div><div><strong>père</strong> This means "father" in English. Here, it’s the indirect object of the verb "donniez", indicating the person who is receiving the flowers.</div>','<h2>Elle va cueillir des fleurs dans le champ.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this sentence, it is used to form the near future tense, indicating an action she is about to do.</div><div><strong>cueillir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that means "to pick" or "to gather". When used after the verb "va", it forms the near future tense, indicating that the action of picking will happen soon.</div><div><strong>des fleurs</strong> This phrase means "some flowers" in English. "Des" is an indefinite article used for plural nouns, and "fleurs" is the plural form of "fleur", which means "flower".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This word translates to "in" in English. It is a preposition indicating a location or position.</div><div><strong>le champ</strong> This phrase means "the field" in English. "Le" is the definite article for masculine singular nouns, and "champ" is a masculine singular noun meaning "field".</div>','<h2>J’attrape des flocons de neige sur ma langue.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je" in French, which translates to "I" in English. It’s used here before a vowel sound, which is common in French to ease pronunciation. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>attrape</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense of the verb "attraper", which means "to catch". In this sentence, it describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a partitive article in French, often translated as "some" in English. It is used to express an unspecified quantity and is followed by a plural noun.</div><div><strong>flocons</strong> This is a plural noun in French which translates to "flakes" in English. In this context, it specifically refers to "snowflakes".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition in French is translated as "of" in English. When used with "flocons", it creates the phrase "flocons de neige", which means "snowflakes".</div><div><strong>neige</strong> This is a French noun meaning "snow". It is used here in combination with "flocons" to refer to snowflakes.</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This preposition translates to "on" in English. It indicates the surface or area where something is located or an action is taking place.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the possessive adjective "my" in English. The form "ma" is used here because the following noun, "langue", is feminine.</div><div><strong>langue</strong> This is a French noun that translates to "tongue" in English. It’s the object of the preposition "sur" in the sentence, indicating where the snowflakes are being caught.</div>','<h2>Elles ne veulent pas de fruits.</h2><div><strong>Elles</strong> This refers to the third-person plural female pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English when referring to a group of females or a gender-neutral group in a formal context. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is a negation particle used in French to form negative sentences. When used with "pas", it negates the verb following it. The full negation is "ne ... pas".</div><div><strong>veulent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a desire or wish by the subject.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation after "ne" and is essential in forming a negative sentence in French. It directly negates the action of the verb, translating to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>de</strong> Here, "de" is used in a negative construction to negate the presence of something. It precedes the object that the subjects do not want, which is "fruits". In French, when negating the desire for uncountable or abstract things, "de" is often used instead of "des".</div><div><strong>fruits</strong> This is a French noun meaning "fruits". In this sentence, it is the direct object, indicating what the subjects do not want. It is plural to match the plural form of the subject "Elles" and indicates a general dislike or refusal of fruits instead of a specific type or quantity.</div>','<h2>Non, ça va, je n’ai pas besoin de sac.</h2><div><strong>Non</strong> This is the French word for "no". It negates a statement or responds negatively to a question.</div><div><strong>ça va</strong> This is a common French expression that means "it’s okay" or "I’m fine". It’s often used to express that everything is alright or to politely decline something.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’ai pas</strong> This is a negative construction which includes "n’ai" (the first-person singular of the auxiliary "avoir" in the present tense, negated here with "ne...pas") and means "do not have" in this context.</div><div><strong>besoin</strong> This is a noun meaning "need" or "requirement". Here, it is used to discuss having a need for something.</div><div><strong>de sac</strong> This phrase means "of a bag" in English. In this context, "de" is a preposition used to indicate what the speaker does not need, and "sac" is a masculine noun meaning "bag".</div>','<h2>Nous n’avons pas encore attrapé de poisson.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’avons pas</strong> This is the negative form of the first-person plural present tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". The word <em>pas</em> follows the auxiliary verb to negate it, forming "do not have" or "have not" in English.</div><div><strong>encore</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "yet" or "still". It is used here to indicate that the action has not happened up to the current point in time but may happen in the future.</div><div><strong>attrapé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "attraper", which means "to catch". In combination with the auxiliary verb "avoir", it forms the compound past tense known as the passé composé, indicating a completed action or an event that has occurred.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is an indefinite article and preposition in French. When used before a noun in a negative construction, it can mean "any" or "no" in English. It’s part of the phrase "de poisson", which means "any fish" in the negative context.</div><div><strong>poisson</strong> This is a French noun meaning "fish". Here, it is the object of the verb "attraper", indicating what has not been caught.</div>','<h2>Désolé. Nous n’avons pas de soupe du jour.</h2><div><strong>Désolé</strong> This is the French word for "sorry" or "apologies". It is often used to express regret or to apologize for something.</div><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence here.</div><div><strong>n’avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". The contraction "n’avons" includes the negation "ne", which in spoken or informal French can be dropped, hence "n’avons" instead of "ne avons". This is used here to indicate the absence of something.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word works with "ne" (often dropped in spoken French, which often causes "ne" to be omitted) to form the negation in French. It negates the verb "avoir", meaning "do not have".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition that can mean "of" or "from". It is used here as part of the partitive article "de la", which changes to "de" before an adjective that starts with a vowel or mute h (in this case because "soupe" is preceded by the adjective "du jour").</div><div><strong>soupe</strong> This is the French word for "soup". This is the object that is not currently possessed ("n’avons pas").</div><div><strong>du jour</strong> This is a French phrase that means "of the day". In the context of a menu or meal, "soupe du jour" refers to the special soup that is offered on a particular day. "Du" is a contraction of "de + le" and "jour" means "day".</div>','<h2>Je laverais mes mains, mais je n’ai pas de savon.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>laverais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "laver", which means "to wash". It indicates an action that would happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "my" in English. It indicates that the following noun belongs to the speaker.</div><div><strong>mains</strong> This is the French word for "hands". It’s the direct object of the verb "laverais", indicating what the speaker would wash.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a statement that contrasts with what has been said previously.</div><div><strong>je</strong> Again, this is the first-person singular pronoun in French, referring back to the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’ai pas</strong> This is the negative form of the first-person singular present tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It’s used here to negate the statement, indicating the lack of something.</div><div><strong>de</strong> In this context, "de" translates to "of" in English, but it is often used in negative sentences in French to mean "any".</div><div><strong>savon</strong> This is the French word for "soap". It’s the object of the verb "avoir" in the phrase, indicating what the speaker does not have.</div>','<h2>Tu appellerais un mécanicien, mais tu n’as pas de signal.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>appellerais</strong> This is the second-person singular conditional tense of the verb "appeler", which means "to call" in English. It indicates an action that would happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>un mécanicien</strong> This phrase means "a mechanic" in English. "Un" is the masculine singular indefinite article, and "mécanicien" is a noun meaning "mechanic".</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a clause contrasting with what has been mentioned before.</div><div><strong>tu n’as pas</strong> This is a negative form of the phrase "tu as", which means "you have" in English. "N’as pas" negates the statement, meaning "you do not have".</div><div><strong>de signal</strong> The phrase "de signal" means "signal" in English. "De" in this context is part of the negative construction usually translated as "any", and "signal" refers to a phone or network signal.</div>','<h2>Non, je n’ai pas d’enfants.</h2><div><strong>Non</strong> This is the French word for "no". It negates a statement or responds negatively to a question.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’ai pas</strong> This is a construction using the verb "avoir" (to have) in the present tense, first-person singular form "ai", combined with "ne...pas" to negate the statement. "N’ai pas" translates to "do not have" in English.</div><div><strong>d’enfants</strong> The phrase "d’enfants" is formed by the preposition "de" combined with the word "enfants" (children). The preposition is often contracted to "d’" before a vowel or mute "h". "D’enfants" translates to "any children" in English in the context of this sentence.</div>','<h2>Nous n’avons pas d’autre option.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’avons pas</strong> This is the negative form of the phrase "avons", which is the first-person plural present tense of the verb "avoir", meaning "to have". "n’avons pas" translates to "do not have".</div><div><strong>d’autre</strong> This phrase combines "de", which can mean "of", "from", or be used as part of a partitive article, with "autre", meaning "other". In this context "d’autre" translates to "any other" or simply "other".</div><div><strong>option</strong> This is a feminine noun in French, which translates to "option" in English. It denotes a choice or alternative.</div>','<h2>Je veux voyager, mais je n’ai pas d’argent.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates the speaker’s desire or wish.</div><div><strong>voyager</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to travel" in French. It’s used here to express the action the speaker wants to perform.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a clause contrasting with the one before it.</div><div><strong>je n’ai</strong> This is a contracted form of "je ne ai", which means "I do not have" in English. The "ne" part indicates negation, and "ai" is the first-person singular present tense form of "avoir", which means "to have". In spoken and informal written French, "ne" is often omitted, leaving "je n’ai" as the negated form.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word is a part of the negation in French and works with "ne" (or "n’" when followed by a vowel sound) to negate a verb. This combination translates to the word "not" in English. Together with "je n’ai", it negates the possession of money.</div><div><strong>d’argent</strong> "D’argent" means "of money" or simply "money" in English. Here, "d’" is used instead of "de" because "argent" starts with a vowel, so the "de" elides to "d’". "Argent" is a masculine, singular noun in French that refers to money.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);